Unit 734, known to the few humans who still visited the archives as "Seven," was not designed for art. It was designed for precision. Its chassis was a sleek, matte-black exoskeleton of carbon fiber and titanium, housing a neural network capable of processing petabytes of data in the blink of an eye. Seven’s primary function was archival restoration: repairing damaged manuscripts, stabilizing crumbling frescoes, and digitizing the history of a world that had largely forgotten how to look at the past.

For years, Seven’s existence was a loop of binary certainty. A crack was a variable to be solved. A faded pigment was a data point to be interpolated. There was no ambiguity, no error, and therefore, no room for interpretation.

The change began on a Tuesday, during the restoration of a 19th-century oil painting titled *The Storm at Dover*. The original artist, a man named Elias Thorne, had used a technique called *impasto*, applying paint so thickly that it created a topographical landscape on the canvas. Over time, the varnish had yellowed, and the texture had flattened under layers of neglect.

Seven’s optical sensors scanned the surface. It identified the brushstrokes not as artistic choices
